<?php declare(strict_types=1); ?><!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Project Unveiled puts the identity of Jesus on trial by separating early evidence from later tradition and showing the source trail."><title>Who Was Jesus? | Project Unveiled — Truth on Trial</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/truth/truth-worthy.css"></head><body><header class="topbar"><div class="wrap nav"><a class="brand" href="/truth/">PROJECT <span>UNVEILED</span></a><nav class="navlinks"><a href="/truth/">Truth on Trial</a><a href="#sources">Source Trail</a><a href="/truth/#ask">Put a Claim on Trial</a><a href="/book/research.html">Research Standards</a></nav></div></header><main><section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">TRUTH ON TRIAL · FLAGSHIP CASE 000003 · POWERED BY TRUST-WORTHY</div><h1>WHO WAS JESUS — BEFORE RELIGION TOLD US WHO HE WAS?</h1><p>Not “What does Christianity say?” Not “What do skeptics say?” This trial starts earlier: What can the surviving record establish, what is interpretation, what is disputed, and what still requires a judgment?</p><div class="manifesto"><strong>FREE FINDING:</strong> The surviving evidence strongly supports a real first-century Jewish Jesus behind the movement that became Christianity. Paul’s letters are earlier than the canonical Gospels and already presuppose Jesus’ execution, followers, and family connections. Later non-Christian writers independently attest that Christians traced their name to a Christus executed under Pontius Pilate and that Jesus was known as the one “called Christ.” That historical core is much easier to establish than the supernatural claims made about him. <strong>YOU BE THE JUDGE.</strong></div></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap grid"><article class="card"><div class="case-head"><div><div class="case-id">PU-TOT-000003</div><h2>The Claim on Trial</h2></div><div class="status">OPEN</div></div><p><strong>Claim:</strong> Jesus of Nazareth was a real first-century Jewish teacher whose execution helped ignite the movement that became Christianity. The harder question—who he ultimately was—requires separating historical evidence, reported testimony, later doctrinal development, philosophical assumptions, and faith claims instead of blending them into one verdict.</p><h2>What survives the first round</h2><ul class="rules"><li><strong>Early Christian evidence:</strong> critical scholarship generally dates Paul’s authentic letters to the 50s CE, earlier than the canonical Gospels. Those letters already treat Jesus as recently crucified and refer to people identified as his followers and kin.</li><li><strong>Paul is not a biography:</strong> his letters were written to communities about specific disputes, so their silence about many episodes in Jesus’ life cannot be treated as proof those episodes never happened.</li><li><strong>The Gospels are later and theological:</strong> they remain indispensable sources, but they must be compared rather than collapsed into a single seamless transcript.</li><li><strong>Tacitus:</strong> writing in the early second century, he connects Christians with Christus and states that Christus suffered the extreme penalty during Tiberius’ reign under Pontius Pilate.</li><li><strong>Josephus:</strong> Antiquities 20 refers to James as the brother of Jesus “who was called Christ.” The authenticity of that shorter James passage is accepted by the large majority of scholars, while Josephus’ longer passage about Jesus in Antiquities 18 is more textually disputed.</li></ul><h2>What this does NOT prove</h2><ul class="rules"><li>A historical Jesus does not automatically prove Christianity true.</li><li>An ancient miracle report does not automatically establish that the miracle occurred.</li><li>Calling Jesus divine does not by itself demonstrate divinity.</li><li>Rejecting miracles as impossible before examining the evidence is also a philosophical assumption, not a historical finding.</li><li>Later church doctrine cannot simply be projected backward and treated as Jesus’ exact vocabulary without testing the development between the sources.</li></ul><h2>The pressure point</h2><p>The shallow debate is “Jesus existed” versus “Jesus was invented.” The more consequential trial begins after existence: <strong>What did the earliest witnesses actually claim, how early are those claims, how independent are they, what changed as the tradition developed, and which explanation best accounts for the movement that appeared afterward?</strong></p><div class="finding"><strong>Provisional verdict:</strong> Historical existence: strongly supported. Execution under Roman authority: strongly supported. Very early resurrection belief: strongly supported as a historical belief held by followers. Resurrection as an actual supernatural event: not established merely by proving the belief existed; that requires a separate trial of testimony, alternatives, prior assumptions, and explanatory power.</div><div class="bobinated"><div class="eyebrow">BOBINATED OPINION · NOT PRESENTED AS PROOF</div><p>The wholesale-invention theory has to explain away too many early anchors while offering less explanatory power than the simpler conclusion that a real Jesus stood behind the movement. The far more interesting—and far harder—question is why people close enough to the beginning became convinced that his execution was not the end. That is where I think the real Truth Trial belongs.</p></div><div class="judge">YOU BE THE JUDGE.<small>READ THE SOURCES · CHALLENGE THE FINDING · FOLLOW THE EVIDENCE</small></div></article><aside class="card"><div class="eyebrow">FREE vs. DEEP DIVE</div><h2>You just got the short investigation.</h2><p>The free result gives the finding, the strongest anchors, the limits of the evidence, and enough source material to check us.</p><div class="finding"><strong>Deep Dive dossier:</strong> Pauline chronology; 1 Corinthians 15 and the early resurrection tradition; Galatians and James/Cephas; Synoptic relationships; John; Josephus 18 and 20; Tacitus; source dependence; contradictions; burial and empty-tomb arguments; hallucination/vision hypotheses; legendary-development models; Jesus’ reported self-understanding; early Christology; strongest skeptical case; strongest Christian case; and what evidence would change the finding.</div><p><strong>Status:</strong> Evidence dossier being expanded.<br><strong>Historical-core confidence:</strong> High.<br><strong>Supernatural verdict:</strong> Separate trial required.</p><p><a class="button" href="/truth/#ask">Challenge This Finding / Submit Evidence</a></p><p class="muted">Trust-Worthy does not ask you to trust a preacher, skeptic, institution, fact-checker, or AI as the final authority. The source trail stays visible so the conclusion can be challenged.</p></aside></div></section><section class="section" id="sources"><div class="wrap"><article class="card"><div class="eyebrow">SOURCE TRAIL · START HERE</div><h2>Check the record for yourself.</h2><ul class="rules"><li><a href="https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Tac.+Ann.+15.44" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Tacitus, Annals 15.44</strong></a> — ancient Roman text connecting Christians with Christus and his execution under Pontius Pilate.</li><li><a href="https://academic.oup.com/book/60034/chapter/513641505" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Josephus, Antiquities 20.200 — Oxford Academic discussion</strong></a> — detailed treatment of the James passage and its authenticity.</li><li><a href="https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/23700/excerpt/9781108423700_excerpt.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>The New Cambridge Companion to St Paul</strong></a> — places most of Paul’s letters in the 50s CE and the canonical Gospels generally a few decades later.</li><li><a href="https://assets.cambridge.org/97805217/67644/excerpt/9780521767644_excerpt.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>J. Albert Harrill, Paul the Apostle — Cambridge</strong></a> — explains why historians distinguish authentic Pauline letters from disputed letters and later sources.</li><li><a href="https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Galatians%201%3A18-20%3B%201%20Corinthians%2015%3A1-8&version=NRSVUE" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Galatians 1:18–20 and 1 Corinthians 15:1–8</strong></a> — primary Christian texts central to the next round of the investigation.</li></ul><p class="muted"><strong>Trust-Worthy note:</strong> A source being ancient, scholarly, Christian, skeptical, or prestigious does not make every conclusion inside it true. Sources are evidence to examine, not authorities granted immunity from challenge.</p></article></div></section></main><footer class="footer"><div class="wrap"><strong>PROJECT UNVEILED</strong> · Truth on Trial · Powered by Trust-Worthy · <strong>YOU BE THE JUDGE.</strong></div></footer></body></html>